Well the first wave of bug reports, suggestions, etc, seems to be tapering
off, so I'll try to address what I can.

First, thanks to all who sent me info about their problems and
suggestions.  I do need input to make this game better for a wider variety
of machines, since I have access to a limited amount here.  So keep
sending me bugs and ideas.

Several people have had a problem with nothing appearing in the window.
Someone discovered on his machine that reversing the initialization of
blackpixel and whitepixel in initx.c seemed to help.  If anyone has done
this, could you send me info on how it looks? Is it inverted? If it is in
color, are the colors strange?

A couple people have been getting BadAccess errors with XStoreColor. At
first I thought that it was trying to allocate all 8 colors on a machine
that has less than 3 planes, but that isn't the case.  I will tinker with
that, and if anyone finds out why that is happening, please let me know.

And now for the biggest goof of all, the memory problem with no blitting.
I feel kind of stupid because I never bothered to check the actual size of
the zones structure.  I have a machine with virtual memory, and under ps
-gux it shows low memory usage since only a small part of the structure is
used and written.  I wasn't thinking about non-VM machines.  In the next
release that will be changed to a dynamic array with a more compact
structure.  I was worried that some machines might be slow at memory
allocation/deallocation, so I wanted everything to be as fast as possible,
and memory is no object.  I can see that that is a bad idea.  For now,
someone posted a nice looking patch that reduces the size of each zone
structure.  However, the array of structures is also unnecessarily big.
The whole thing only stores the zones of the onscreen objects, so having
it be zones[MAXOBJECTS] is pointless.  Changing to zones[100] and
touch[20] should be perfectly ok for now.  I could post a patch here if
there is enough demand.  I think my problem is that I have been taking too
many courses in theoretical cs, where not allowing for all objects to be
on the screen at once would get me an F. :)

On a lighter note, two people have expressed an interest in special
levels.  If you want to play with this, the code that reads in each level
is in special.c.  It uses fscanf to read in a series of integers and
strings from a file.  If you look at the code and at the layout of the
provided demo level, you should be able to pick it up quickly.  I will
probably change the code a bit to allow randomness in various parameters,
but if you want to craft some levels and send them to me, I might include
them with the next release.  If you have a great idea for a level, but
don't have time to implement it, send me a description.

Thanks again for all the input, and keep sending me suggestions.
